---
/**
 * Google Consent Mode v2 defaults. THIS RUNS FIRST, IN THE HEAD, BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE.
 *
 * It is inline and it has no imports for one reason: it has to have already executed
 * before any tag could possibly be in a position to read consent. A bundled module is
 * fetched, and anything fetched is a race. There is no race worth having here — if the
 * defaults land late, a tag reads "no signal" instead of "denied", and the difference
 * between those two is the whole point of Consent Mode.
 *
 * THE THREE ADVERTISING SIGNALS DEFAULT TO DENIED — `ad_storage`, `ad_user_data`,
 * `ad_personalization`. Nothing advertising-related is granted on a first visit.
 *
 * `analytics_storage` DEFAULTS TO GRANTED, and that is not a mistake to be tidied.
 * Fredrick's instruction of 27 August 2026: analytics is treated as necessary, loads on
 * the first page view before any interaction, and cannot be switched off. The signal has
 * to match what actually happens — a denied default here with Google Analytics loading
 * anyway would be a page telling Google one thing and doing another, which is worse than
 * either position taken honestly. See the note on CONSENT_CATEGORIES in lib/consent.ts
 * for what that decision cost elsewhere.
 *
 * A RETURNING VISITOR'S GRANT IS APPLIED HERE TOO, not later in the gate. Consent Mode
 * wants the update as early as it can get it, and reading one localStorage key is cheap
 * enough to do in the head. The gate re-reads the same key when it runs; the two agree
 * because the key and the version stamp are injected from the same module both use.
 *
 * MICROSOFT CLARITY IS NOT REPRESENTED HERE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Clarity is gated by
 * not loading its script at all, rather than by loading it and asking it to behave.
 * Simpler, and stronger: a script that is absent cannot record.
 *
 * Cal.com is also absent from this file, and that is a decision rather than an
 * oversight. The booking embed sits outside the consent gate entirely — it loads for
 * every visitor because booking a call is what this site is for, and the visitor asked
 * for it. It is not analytics, not advertising and not recording. What IS gated is the
 * conversion event a completed booking fires. See BaseLayout.
 */
import { CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY, CONSENT_COPY_VERSION } from "../../lib/consent";
---

<script
  is:inline
  define:vars={{ key: CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY, version: CONSENT_COPY_VERSION }}
>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag() {
    window.dataLayer.push(arguments);
  }
  window.gtag = gtag;

  gtag("consent", "default", {
    ad_storage: "denied",
    ad_user_data: "denied",
    ad_personalization: "denied",
    analytics_storage: "granted",
  });

  /* A stored choice, applied before anything can look. Guarded throughout: localStorage
   * throws rather than returning null in some privacy modes, and a browser that will
   * not let us remember the choice must still get the denied defaults above rather than
   * an exception that stops the rest of the head. */
  try {
    var raw = window.localStorage.getItem(key);
    if (raw) {
      var stored = JSON.parse(raw);
      /* A record stamped with older banner copy is not consent to the copy showing
       * today, so it is ignored here exactly as the gate ignores it. */
      if (stored && stored.v === version && stored.c) {
        var advertising = stored.c.advertising === true ? "granted" : "denied";
        /* analytics_storage is not read from the record. It is locked on, so a stored
         * false — from an older record or a hand-edited entry — must not downgrade it. */
        gtag("consent", "update", {
          analytics_storage: "granted",
          ad_storage: advertising,
          ad_user_data: advertising,
          ad_personalization: advertising,
        });
      }
    }
  } catch (e) {
    /* Unreadable or unparseable storage means no current consent, which is already the
     * state set above. Nothing to do, and nothing worth telling the visitor. */
  }
</script>
